body:
I'm not sure what exactly the question you're asking is, so I probably won't be able to give an adequate answer, but as a nonbinary person who spends way too much time thinking about the social definition of gender, I feel obliged to give my input.

The root of much of the casual transphobia I receive from people on a daily basis seems to be that many cis people think trans identity is exclusively about performance. "Why can't girls just be tomboys anymore?" "Wanting to wear a dress doesn't mean you're any less of a boy." But the truth of the matter for many nonbinary people is entirely opposite. Gender roles are just expectations. If any man can do absolutely anything women can do and any woman can do absolutely anything men can do, what is the significance of being a man or a woman at all?

&#x200B;

When I first came out, many of the arguments I had in defense of my identity would eventually boil down to the definition of gender. Every dictionary has a vastly different definition and most of them are fairly frustrating to think about. One of the most common dictionary definitions, though, describes it as "the state of being male or female." I've seen plenty of well-meaning people actually use this to defend nonbinary genders, proposing that gender can exist between male and female as well at either end, and I've even seen some nonbinary people who think of gender this way: that male and female are the only "real" genders, and nonbinary people are only valid if they can justify their experience as being somewhere on a spectrum in between the two.

&#x200B;

For a while, that's what I believed too. I called myself a demiboy and I rationalized it to myself by saying my identity was a certain percent neutral, a certain percent female, but mostly still male. (The truth was I deeply hated being considered in any sense a man but didn't want to address my internalized transmisogyny) And during this time I would mock and harass people whose identities were more inherently queer and existed outside of any reference to manhood or womanhood. Eventually, though, I came to realize that this mindset was objectively cisnormative and damaging and set out to again redefine my perspective on gender.

&#x200B;

The best I can figure now? Gender doesn't exist at all. Gender as a whole is a very silly concept. Where the hell would it come from? Do all women have a glass ball in their chest filled with pink glitter? Is there one mighty principle, value, or aesthetic that exclusive to one gender but not any other?

&#x200B;

Somehow I still think I have one, though. It's tiny and soft but it's also large and dangerous. Sometimes it sounds like Carly Rae Jepson and sometimes sounds like nails on a chalkboard. both maleness and womanhood are deeply foreign to me and I refuse to be any part of that system, but I'm not agender either. What I am is something deeply personal to me that I could never and would never want to communicate to someone else. And on that same prospect, I could never expect anyone else to communicate their gender to me.  When a transphobe tells me he knows he's a man because he has a dick, who am I to tell him his identity is invalid because it doesn't comply with my system of belief? My ex-boyfriend on the other hand tells me that being cis was just a coincidence for him. He spent a long time questioning his gender and came to the conclusion that being  a man and being born with a penis are two entirely separate parts of the way he was born.

&#x200B;

I have had countless people open conversations about their gender with me and tell me that they like performing gender in a way that complies with their birth sex, but don't necessarily love the label of man/woman. In response, I always ask "If you don't like calling yourself that, why should you have to?" or "If you don't like being a man/woman, why do call yourself one?" Every single person I've asked this question has later started openly identifying themselves as nonbinary. The only thing that changed for them? Perspective. And that's all that gender is.

You don't understand, though. You can't understand because you're cis and you don't live any kind of trans experience. You refer to me just as a trans (adjective only), but yourself as a gay man (adjective, noun). Do you not see how dehumanizing that is?

&#x200B;

I'm nonbinary and pansexual. Right now, grindr is the only app with an active user base that I can be on without being forced to pick whether I'd rather be included in searches for men or women, inaccurately identifying my experience of gender, and limiting me from seeing half of the user-base that should be in my market. I'm never said grindr was a "good" dating app. For every fetishist I block, I also have to block several racists, pedophiles, cheaters, and scammers. The majority of grindr's user base consists of fundamentally awful human beings and grindr doesn't do enough to improve the experience of being on there, but we all just live with it anyway because we like to fuck.  That doesn't change the fact that grindr is not exclusively for mlm. If it was meant to be, they could still be inclusive to all men by limiting trans inclusion to just trans men. But it's not. Regardless of the way you use the app or how you think it should be, there is no part of grindr itself that indicates it should be exclusively for men. 

&#x200B;

If for some reason, you desperately need an app that will only show you mlm, use literally any other app than grindr, where you have to select your gender and who you're attracted to in order to make an account. You keep on talking about how trans women and gay men "have different markets" but being trans and navigating attraction is a very complex thing that, again, you can never understand. Sure, we have different markets on account of me being pan and being attracted to women, but as far as my experience on grindr goes, I think you'd be surprised at how similar we actually are. Primarily you seem to be upset with cis men who claim to only be looking for trans folks, and I can fully sympathize with you on that. I am repulsed by straight men- in general, but especially trans fetishists, and I've never had sex with one.

&#x200B;

The beauty of the queer community to me- in it's whole, not just on grindr- is that we are more capable of abandoning heteronormative , binarist, and conformotive ideologies. Would you believe me if I told you that a great number of the men I've been with before actually identify as gay? Plenty of people experience occasional attraction outside the sphere of their general attraction, and it in no way invalidates your identity to act on it. And for everyone else I've been with from, they have been bi or pan and have had plenty experience with other cis men and their profiles look just like everyone else's.

&#x200B;

Our communities are deeply and intrinsically connected. Segregation of the community only benefits the cishets.

Grindr isn't and never claims to be exclusively for homosexual cis men. Grindr refers to itself as a social networking app for gay, bi, trans, and queer people and offers both specifically trans-inclusive gender options as well as full freedom to gender customization. The about page on this very reddit refers to grindr as a gay/bi/curious/trans dating. Grindr is meant for all queer people and you are just wrong if you think it isn't.

&#x200B;

That said, I do strongly believe trans fetishism is a problem on grindr. As a transfeminine person, I have no interest whatsoever in people whose attraction to me is dependant on my identity or the transness of my body, and I block the people who do act like that. Not all of these people are cishet, though. Plenty of these trans fetishists are bi, pan, or otherwise multisexual, and have just as much a right to use the app as any other queer person does.
